Agent: That’s a meaningful research topic — and there are several focused routes you can take to learn more about your great‑aunt’s experience and about refugee/resettlement processes around WWII. Below I list practical steps, key repositories and databases, what information to collect first, and some tips on making requests or getting professional help.

1) Gather what you already know (best first step)
- Full name (including maiden name and any alternate spellings)  
- Date and place of birth (or approximate year/place)  
- Parents’ names (if known) and any siblings  
- Dates, ports, or approximate year of departure from Europe and arrival in the U.S.  
- Where she settled in the U.S. (city/state), any naturalization date or SSN, and any known refugee/assistance organizations she used  
- Copies or photos of any documents (passports, naturalization papers, passenger tickets, letters, photos with captions) and family oral history notes

2) U.S. records and starting places
- USCIS Genealogy Program — request immigration, naturalization, or file copies (A‑file). Good if she later naturalized in the U.S. (there is a fee and processing time).  
- National Archives (NARA) — passenger arrival lists (e.g., for certain ports/years), naturalization records, and other federal records. Use their online catalog and AAD (Access to Archival Databases) for passenger manifests and refugee lists.  
- Ellis Island / Castle Garden databases — if arrival was via New York (use name variants).  
- Local county courthouses / state archives where she lived — naturalization petitions, marriage licenses, death certificates often contain immigration details.  
- Social Security Death Index / Social Security application (SS‑5) — SS‑5 sometimes records birthplace and parents’ names (available via request or genealogy services).

3) European / German records
- Standesamt (civil registry) and Kirchenbuch (church/parish) records for the town where she was born — these have births, baptisms, marriages. For wartime births this can be tricky but often invaluable.  
- German Federal Archives (Bundesarchiv) — wartime and postwar administrative records; some refugee/DP era files.  
- Local archives (Stadtarchives, Kreis archives) in the town/county of origin — they may hold municipal or police records.

4) Displaced Persons (DP) and refugee-specific sources
- International Tracing Service / Arolsen Archives (ITS) — major repository for displaced persons, concentration camp records, and postwar DP camps. They have an online search and provide copies for legitimate family research requests. (Important for refugees and displaced persons after WWII.)  
- International Committee of the Red Cross (ICRC) tracing archives — they helped reunify families and may have relevant case files.  
- UNRRA and IRO records — archives hold postwar migration and resettlement records.

5) Holocaust and wartime victim / survivor resources (if applicable)
- U.S. Holocaust Memorial Museum — collections, oral histories, transport lists, and databases that can include refugee information.  
- Yad Vashem and other memorial archives (if relevant to the family story).

6) Genealogy & commercial databases (good for fast searching)
- Ancestry.com, FamilySearch.org, MyHeritage, Findmypast — passenger lists, naturalizations, census records, draft registrations, city directories, and indexed European records. FamilySearch is free and often has scanned European parish records.  
- Fold3 — military and some immigration records.  
(Use wildcard/alternate spelling searches; search maiden and married names.)

7) Local U.S. helpers & oral history
- Interview relatives (record audio) to capture dates, stories, nicknames, towns, and any documents they may hold. Even small details (a ship name, sponsor’s name, prayer card) can unlock records.  
- Local historical societies in towns she lived in — they sometimes have immigration-focused collections or local clippings.

8) Practical tips for requests & searches
- Be exact: when requesting records from archives (USCIS, ITS, NARA), include full name variations, birth year, approximate immigration date, and the place they settled.  
- Expect fees and wait times — USCIS and ITS charge and may take several weeks/months.  
- Document every search: keep a research log of places searched, dates, people/terms used, and results (even negatives).  
- If stuck, consider hiring a professional genealogist or researcher (look for those who specialize in German records and refugee/DP work).

9) Sensitivity & privacy
- Be mindful of privacy and the sensitive nature of wartime/refugee records. Some archives have restrictions; staff can advise best approaches.

10) I can help with next steps
If you’d like, I can:  
- Draft sample requests/queries for USCIS, ITS, or a German Standesamt;  
- Suggest exact search terms and wildcard patterns to use on Ancestry/FamilySearch; or  
- Help you organize the information you already have into a searchable format.

Tell me which part you want to tackle first (e.g., draft an USCIS request, search passenger manifests, or prepare a list of family questions), and I’ll help you draft the wording and next actions.
